Run this check first to confirm which disk latency metrics exist in your tenant:

fetch metric.series, from:-2h
| filter startsWith(metric.key, "dt.host.disk")
| summarize series = count(), by:{metric.key}


This draft assumes dt.host.disk.read_time, write_time and queue_length exist. Adjust it after the check.

Visual: Table. The 20 ms latency threshold is a starting value.

timeseries {r = avg(dt.host.disk.read_time), w = avg(dt.host.disk.write_time),
            q = avg(dt.host.disk.queue_length)},
           by:{dt.entity.host, dt.entity.disk}
| fieldsAdd readMs = round(arrayMax(r), decimals:1),
            writeMs = round(arrayMax(w), decimals:1),
            queue = round(arrayMax(q), decimals:1)
| filter readMs >= 20 or writeMs >= 20
| lookup [ fetch dt.entity.host | fields id, hostName = entity.name, tags ],
         sourceField: dt.entity.host, lookupField: id, prefix: "h."
| lookup [ fetch dt.entity.disk | fields id, mount = entity.name ],
         sourceField: dt.entity.disk, lookupField: id, prefix: "d."
| expand tag = h.tags
| filter startsWith(tag, "APMID:")
| fieldsAdd apmid = splitString(tag, "APMID:")[1]
| filter in(apmid, array($ClaimsApps))
| fields apmid, host = h.hostName, mount = d.mount, readMs, writeMs, queue
| sort writeMs desc


